<p>Non-resident Indians (NRIs) can apply for Indian IPOs much like resident investors. The difference is in the accounts: the money must come from an NRI bank account, and the shares go to an NRI demat account. This guide covers the accounts you need, how to apply, which category to bid in, and how tax and repatriation work.</p>

<h2 id="accounts">Accounts an NRI needs</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>What it is for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>NRE account</strong> (Non-Resident External)</td><td>Money earned abroad. Investments made from it are <strong>repatriable</strong>: the sale proceeds can be sent back abroad freely.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>NRO account</strong> (Non-Resident Ordinary)</td><td>Money earned in India, such as rent or dividends. Investments made from it are <strong>non-repatriable</strong>, with limits on sending money abroad.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>NRI demat and trading account</strong></td><td>Holds the allotted shares and lets you sell them. Repatriable and non-repatriable holdings need separate demat accounts, each linked to the matching NRE or NRO account.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>PAN</strong></td><td>Required for every IPO application, as for residents.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>PIS (Portfolio Investment Scheme):</strong> a permission from a designated bank that NRIs generally need to buy and sell listed shares on the stock exchange on a repatriable basis. Many banks and brokers do not ask for it just to apply in an IPO, but you will usually need it to sell the shares later. Rules differ between banks, so check with yours before you apply.</p>

<h2 id="apply">How NRIs apply for an IPO</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Choose the account.</strong> Use your NRE account for a repatriable investment or your NRO account for a non-repatriable one.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply through ASBA.</strong> Log in to net banking of the bank that holds your NRE or NRO account, open the IPO or ASBA section and choose the IPO.</li>
    <li><strong>Select the NRI investor type.</strong> Pick "NRI – repatriable" or "NRI – non-repatriable" to match the account, and enter the demat account linked to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose lots and bid.</strong> Enter the number of lots and bid at the cut-off price if you are applying as a retail investor.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm before the deadline.</strong> The amount is blocked in your account and debited only if you get shares. Bids close at 5 PM Indian time on the last day.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>UPI:</strong> some banks allow UPI on NRE and NRO accounts, including with certain international mobile numbers. If yours does, you can also apply through a broker app with a UPI mandate. Otherwise ASBA net banking is the way to apply. For the general steps, see <a href="{{ route('guides.show', 'how-to-apply-for-ipo') }}">how to apply for an IPO</a>.</p>

<h2 id="category">Which category can NRIs bid in?</h2>
<p>NRIs bid in the same categories as resident individuals: <strong>retail</strong> for applications up to ₹2 lakh and <strong>non-institutional (NII)</strong> above ₹2 lakh. The same allotment rules apply, including the lottery for retail applicants in oversubscribed issues. NRIs can bid in employee or shareholder quotas only if they are eligible for them. See <a href="{{ route('guides.show', 'how-ipo-allotment-works') }}">how IPO allotment works</a>.</p>

<h2 id="tax">Tax on IPO gains for NRIs</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Rates:</strong> the same as for residents. Shares sold within 12 months are taxed as short-term capital gains at 20%; long-term gains above ₹1.25 lakh a year are taxed at 12.5%. Surcharge and 4% cess apply on top.</li>
    <li><strong>TDS:</strong> tax on capital gains is deducted at source when an NRI sells shares, usually by the broker or bank.</li>
    <li><strong>Tax treaties:</strong> a lower rate may apply under the tax treaty (DTAA) between India and your country of residence, if you have a Tax Residency Certificate.</li>
    <li><strong>Returns:</strong> filing an Indian income tax return lets you claim a refund if more tax was deducted than you owe.</li>
</ul>
<p>More detail in our guide to <a href="{{ route('guides.show', 'ipo-listing-gains-tax') }}">tax on IPO listing gains</a>.</p>

<h2 id="repatriation">Sending the money abroad</h2>
<p>Proceeds from shares held on a repatriable basis (bought through an NRE account) can be credited back to the NRE account and sent abroad freely. Proceeds from non-repatriable holdings go to the NRO account; money in an NRO account can be repatriated up to USD 1 million per financial year after tax, with the forms your bank asks for.</p>

<h2 id="checklist">Checklist before you apply</h2>
<ul>
    <li>NRE or NRO account with ASBA or UPI enabled</li>
    <li>NRI demat and trading account linked to the same bank account</li>
    <li>PIS permission if your bank or broker needs it to sell the shares later</li>
    <li>PAN, with your NRI status updated with your bank and broker</li>
    <li>Enough balance to cover the full bid amount until allotment</li>
</ul>
<p class="callout">Banking and FEMA requirements for NRIs vary between banks and change from time to time. This guide explains general rules and is not tax or legal advice; confirm the details with your bank, broker or a chartered accountant.</p>
